<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Thomas Becket</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1119–<a href="/years/1170">1170</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> · Also known as Saint Thomas of Canterbury, Thomas of London, Thomas à Becket</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Thomas Becket was an English cleric and statesman who served as Lord Chancellor from 1155 to 1162 and as Archbishop of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canterbury">Canterbury</a> from 1162 until his murder in 1170. Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> to a Norman family, he rose in the household of Archbishop Theobald of Bec, who recommended him to Henry II. As archbishop he resisted the king's attempt to reassert royal prerogatives over the Church; convicted in 1164 of contempt of royal authority, he spent six years in exile under the protection of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-vii-of-france">Louis VII of France</a>. Soon after his return in 1170 four knights loyal to Henry killed him in Canterbury Cathedral. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-alexander-iii">Pope Alexander III</a> canonised him in 1173, and he is venerated as a saint and martyr.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thomas-becket.jpg" alt="Thomas Becket" width="238" height="357" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown Miniaturist, English (active 1170s in Cirenchester) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:London._British_Library,_Cotton_MS_Claudius_B_II._f.341r_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Becket was born at Cheapside in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, on 21 December, the feast day of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thomas-the-apostle">Thomas the Apostle</a>. His parents were Gilbert and Matilda Beket, both of Norman descent. Gilbert began as a merchant and later lived on the rents of London properties and served as the city's sheriff. At the age of ten Becket was sent to Merton Priory in Surrey, and he later attended a grammar school in London. When his father suffered financial reverses he worked as a clerk, and later he entered the household of Archbishop Theobald of Bec.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Theobald sent him on missions to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> and to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bologna">Bologna</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/auxerre">Auxerre</a> to study canon law, and in 1154 made him Archdeacon of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canterbury">Canterbury</a>. As chancellor he enforced the king's traditional revenues, including those owed by churches. After Theobald's death Becket was confirmed as archbishop on 23 May 1162, ordained priest on 2 June and consecrated the next day. He resigned the chancellorship and sought to recover and extend the rights of the archbishopric, to the king's displeasure.</p><p>At Clarendon Palace on 30 January 1164 Henry presented sixteen constitutions to limit clerical independence and weaken connections with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>. All the clergy agreed except Becket, who still refused to sign. Summoned to Northampton Castle on 8 October 1164 to answer charges of contempt of royal authority and malfeasance as chancellor, he was convicted and fled to the Continent. Louis VII of France protected him. He spent nearly two years at the Cistercian abbey of Pontigny, until Henry's threats against the order sent him to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sens">Sens</a>. Pope Alexander III favoured diplomacy, and in 1170 Henry offered a compromise that allowed Becket to return.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 1170 the Archbishop of York crowned the heir, Henry the Young King, which breached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canterbury">Canterbury</a>'s privilege, and Becket excommunicated those involved. Henry II is said to have spoken words that his men took as a wish to have Becket killed, and the exact wording is in doubt. Four knights, Reginald FitzUrse, Hugh de Morville, William de Tracy and Richard le Breton, came to Canterbury on 29 December 1170 and killed Becket in the cathedral. The eyewitness Edward Grim, who was wounded, left an account.</p><p>Veneration began across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> at once, and Alexander III canonised him on 21 February 1173. On 12 July 1174 Henry did public penance at his tomb. In 1220 his bones were moved to a gold-plated shrine in the Trinity Chapel, which Henry VIII destroyed in 1538. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geoffrey-chaucer">Geoffrey Chaucer</a>'s Canterbury Tales is set among pilgrims travelling to it.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Thomas Becket” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Learning and royal service could carry a man of modest birth to these offices: <b>Thomas Becket</b>, a London merchant’s son, became Archbishop of Canterbury in 1162.</blockquote><a href="/societies/feudal-europe">Social Hierarchy of Feudal Europe</a></li><li><blockquote>It came after Henry's agents destroyed the shrine of <b>Thomas Becket</b> at Canterbury.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/december-17">17 December in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Four knights, believing they were carrying out Henry II's wishes, killed Archbishop <b>Thomas Becket</b> in his cathedral.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/december-29">29 December in history</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Cheapside, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. Died in Canterbury Cathedral, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=51.51,-0.09&amp;year=1152" aria-label="Cheapside on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4548 381 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Thomas Becket"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4998 651h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4998 651h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=51.51,-0.09&amp;year=1152">What was here in 1152? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
